Specification Transactions

Description The transaction, its postings, and the rule that every transaction
Description balances. Splits and transfers are not separate kinds of thing here:
Description they are what a transaction looks like with three postings, or with
Description two postings to real accounts.
Details
Details Tables in this file group postings into transactions with a Ref column.
Details Ref is how a table says that two rows belong together, and nothing more
Details than that: a device of these tables, short enough to read. It is not
Details shown anywhere in the program and it is not what a transaction is stored
Details under. That is its Id, given once when the transaction is created and
Details never changed afterwards. See the rule on naming a transaction.

DataType TransactionRef
Description How a table in this specification says which postings belong to one
Description transaction. Not the stored identity, which is the Id.
Uses Text
Examples: ValidValues
| Value | IsValid | Notes                               |
| T1    | true    | Short names are used in these tables |
| 4091  | true    | The program assigns numbers         |
|       | false   | A posting always belongs to one     |

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The shape of a transaction
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

Entity Posting
Description One side of a transaction: an amount against one account.
Details Debits are positive and credits are negative, for every account type.
Details See the display sign rule in Accounts for how this is read back out.
| Attribute | Type          | Default   | Notes                            |
| Account   | AccountPath   |           | Any account, real or nominal     |
| Amount    | Money         | 0.00      | Positive is a debit              |
| Memo      | Text          | none      | Per side, for a split line       |
| Cleared   | ClearedStatus | Uncleared | Per side; a transfer clears twice |

Collection Postings
Description The sides of one transaction.
| DataType | Minimum | Maximum | Notes                       |
| Posting  | 2       | 200     | The amounts must sum to zero |

DataType ImportSource
Description Where an identifier came from. Two files describing one transaction
Description do not agree on what to call it, so the name of the source travels
Description with the name of the transaction.
Examples: EnumerationValues
| Value | Notes                                      |
| Ofx   | A FITID, from an OFX or QFX download        |
| Csv   | Whatever the bank's CSV calls a reference   |
| Qif   | From a QIF migration, where one is present |

Entity ImportId
Description An identifier a file gave to one posting, and which file gave it.
Details Per posting rather than per transaction, because a download describes
Details one side of it: the bank knows what left the chequing account and has
Details never heard of the category it was spent on.
| Attribute | Type         | Default | Notes                                |
| Source    | ImportSource | Ofx     | Which kind of file said so           |
| Id        | Text         |         | As that file gave it, unchanged      |

Collection ImportIds
Description The identifiers one posting has collected. More than one, because a
Description posting imported from a CSV and later recognised in a QFX has a name
Description in each, and losing either would make that file import twice.
| DataType | Minimum | Maximum | Notes                        |
| ImportId | 0       | 8       | One per source at most       |

Entity Transaction
Description What the two or more postings share.
| Attribute | Type           | Default    | Notes                        |
| Id        | TransactionId  | none       | Identity; given once, never changes. These tables never name one: it is given by whatever creates the transaction, so a table that stated one would be stating something no scenario controls |
| Ref       | TransactionRef |            | A device of these tables; not stored |
| Date      | Date           | 2024-01-01 | The date it is posted on     |
| Payee     | PayeeName      | none       | Optional                     |
| CheckNo   | CheckNumber    | none       | Optional                     |
| Memo      | Text           | none       | For the transaction as a whole |
| Tag       | Text           | none       | A Quicken class, kept on migration; see ImportQif |

Attributes PostingRow
Description A posting with its transaction, which is how these tables are written.
| Attribute | Type           | Default   |
| Ref       | TransactionRef |           |
| Date      | Date           | 2024-01-01 |
| Payee     | PayeeName      | none      |
| Account   | AccountPath    |           |
| Amount    | Money          | 0.00      |
| Memo      | Text           | none      |
| Cleared   | ClearedStatus  | Uncleared |

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The two kinds of unknown
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

BusinessRule An unknown category and an unknown account are different unknowns
Description A charge whose category nobody has chosen is Uncategorized. A payment
Description whose other account nobody has worked out is Unassigned. They are
Description separate because they are different questions with different answers
Description and different ways of being resolved.
Details Uncategorized asks what the money was for. It is answered by choosing a
Details category, by a payee rule, or by leaving it: a spending report with a
Details line called Uncategorized is telling the truth about money that was
Details spent on something nobody classified, and Quicken's own reports have had
Details such a line for twenty years.
Details
Details Unassigned asks which account the money went to. It is answered by the
Details other side of the transfer turning up in a later import, or by a person
Details assigning it in the register. It is never answered by leaving it, because
Details money that left one account and arrived nowhere is not a fact about the
Details world -- it is a fact about what has been imported so far.
Details
Details That difference is why Unassigned is a real account and Uncategorized is
Details a category. Unassigned holds money in transit, its balance is the work
Details outstanding, and that balance is expected to reach zero. A book whose
Details Unassigned balance is not zero is not wrong: it is telling its owner how
Details many halves of transfers are still waiting for the other half.
Examples: TwoUnknowns
| TheUnknown     | TheQuestion                      | WhereItLives | ClearedBy                        |
| Uncategorized  | What was the money for           | A category   | A category, a payee rule, or left |
| Unassigned     | Which account did the money go to | A real account | The other import, or the register |

Attributes TwoUnknowns
| Attribute   | Type   | Default |
| TheUnknown  | Text   |         |
| TheQuestion | String |         |
| WhereItLives | Text  |         |
| ClearedBy   | String |         |

Scenario Assigning the other account in the register empties Unassigned
Description The last resort, and the one that always works. A payment nothing
Description matched is sitting with its other side in Unassigned; the user picks
Description the account in the register and the suspense balance goes to zero.
Given the chart of accounts is : ChartOfAccounts
| Path                   | Type       | Placeholder |
| Assets:Checking        | Bank       | false       |
| Liabilities:Chase Visa | CreditCard | false       |
| Unassigned             | Asset      | false       |
Given postings are : PostingRow
| Ref | Date       | Payee     | Account         | Amount   | Memo | Cleared   |
| T1  | 2026-04-10 | CHASEBANK | Assets:Checking | -1200.00 | none | Cleared   |
| T1  | 2026-04-10 | CHASEBANK | Unassigned      | 1200.00  | none | Uncleared |
When the other account is assigned : AccountAssignment Vertical
| Ref     | T1                     |
| Account | Liabilities:Chase Visa |
Then postings are : PostingRow
| Ref | Date       | Payee     | Account                | Amount   | Memo | Cleared   |
| T1  | 2026-04-10 | CHASEBANK | Assets:Checking        | -1200.00 | none | Cleared   |
| T1  | 2026-04-10 | CHASEBANK | Liabilities:Chase Visa | 1200.00  | none | Uncleared |
Then balances are : AccountBalance CompareOnly
| Account    | RawBalance |
| Unassigned | 0.00       |
# The posting was not added and nothing was deleted: the one posting that said
# Unassigned now says which account, which is the whole of the change. It stays
# uncleared, because the card has still not reported it.

Attributes AccountAssignment
Description Assigning the other account of a transfer by hand, in the register.
| Attribute | Type           | Default |
| Ref       | TransactionRef |         |
| Account   | AccountPath    |         |

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Money from outside the book
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

BusinessRule Whether the other side is an account or a category
Description The other side of a downloaded row is an account when the money came
Description from or went to something this book keeps, and a category when it did
Description not. That single question decides which of the two unknowns is used,
Description and nothing else about the row decides it.
Details A payment from the bank to a credit card the book keeps is a transfer and
Details nothing else: both halves are accounts here, the money never left the
Details book, and the two halves add to nothing. The book balances because it
Details should -- that is the whole of double entry and there is no income or
Details expense anywhere in it.
Details
Details A paycheck is not a transfer. The money came from an employer this book
Details does not keep, so there is no second account for it to have come out of,
Details and the other side is a category: Income:Salary. The same goes for a
Details royalty, and for a distribution from an IRA or a trust that the book does
Details not itself hold -- money arriving from outside is income, however
Details institutional the sender.
Details
Details This is why a payee that matches no account's PaymentPayee is not a
Details failed transfer. It is an ordinary deposit, and recording it as a
Details category is correct rather than second best. Unassigned is only for a row
Details that is known to have another side in this book and does not yet know
Details which.
Examples: WhereTheOtherSideLives
| TheRow                                | TheOtherSide          | Because                               |
| A payment to a card the book keeps    | The card account      | Both halves are in the book           |
| A transfer between two bank accounts  | The other bank account | Both halves are in the book          |
| A paycheck                            | An income category    | The employer is not in the book       |
| A royalty                             | An income category    | The publisher is not in the book      |
| A distribution from an IRA not held here | An income category | The IRA is not in the book            |
| A distribution from an IRA held here  | The IRA account       | It is in the book, so it is a transfer |
| A card charge                         | An expense category   | The shop is not in the book           |

Attributes WhereTheOtherSideLives
| Attribute    | Type   | Default |
| TheRow       | String |         |
| TheOtherSide | String |         |
| Because      | String |         |

Scenario A deposit from outside the book is a category, not an unassigned transfer
Description A paycheck. Nothing in the book is the other side of it, so there is
Description nothing for Unassigned to be waiting for and the row is categorised
Description like any other.
Given the chart of accounts is : ChartOfAccounts
| Path                 | Type   | Placeholder |
| Assets:Checking      | Bank   | false       |
| Income:Uncategorized | Income | false       |
| Unassigned           | Asset  | false       |
Given postings are : PostingRow
| Ref | Date       | Payee        | Account              | Amount   | Memo | Cleared   |
| T1  | 2026-04-15 | ACME PAYROLL | Assets:Checking      | 3000.00  | none | Cleared   |
| T1  | 2026-04-15 | ACME PAYROLL | Income:Uncategorized | -3000.00 | none | Uncleared |
Then balances are : AccountBalance CompareOnly
| Account    | RawBalance |
| Unassigned | 0.00       |
# Nothing is waiting. Choosing Income:Salary later is a category being chosen,
# not a transfer being completed, and the book balanced all along.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Matching an entry in another register by hand
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

BusinessRule Assigning an account asks whether the other entry already exists
Description Assigning a real account to an Unassigned side says where the money
Description went. It does not say whether that account already has a row for it.
Description Both are possible and they are different outcomes, so the program
Description asks rather than choosing.
Details Making a new entry is right when the other account has no row for this
Details money -- usually because it has not been imported yet. The transfer
Details becomes one transaction with two real postings and the second account
Details will recognise its own row when its file is read.
Details
Details Matching an existing entry is right when the other account already has
Details the row, entered by hand or imported too long ago or too far out for the
Details windows to have paired them. Then there are two transactions describing
Details one movement of money, and leaving both would double it. They become one.
Details
Details So the register offers the other account's entries to scroll through,
Details nearest in date and amount first, and the user picks the one that is the
Details same movement of money. Picking nothing and making a new entry instead is
Details always available, because a user who cannot find it is better served by a
Details new row than by being made to choose wrongly.
Examples: AssignmentOutcome
| TheOtherAccount                | WhatHappens                                        |
| Has no row for this money      | A new entry is made there; one transaction, two postings |
| Has the row, found by the user | The two become one transaction; nothing is doubled  |
| Has the row, not found         | A new entry is made; the duplicate is left to be seen later |

Attributes AssignmentOutcome
| Attribute       | Type   | Default |
| TheOtherAccount | String |         |
| WhatHappens     | String |         |

Scenario Matching an entry in the other register joins two transactions into one
Description The card was imported weeks ago and the payment never paired, because
Description the dates were further apart than the window allows. Both sides are
Description in the book as separate transactions. Matching them makes one.
Given the chart of accounts is : ChartOfAccounts
| Path                   | Type       | Placeholder |
| Assets:Checking        | Bank       | false       |
| Liabilities:Chase Visa | CreditCard | false       |
| Unassigned             | Asset      | false       |
Given postings are : PostingRow
| Ref | Date       | Payee            | Account                | Amount   | Memo | Cleared   |
| T1  | 2026-04-10 | CHASEBANK        | Assets:Checking        | -1200.00 | none | Cleared   |
| T1  | 2026-04-10 | CHASEBANK        | Unassigned             | 1200.00  | none | Uncleared |
| T2  | 2026-04-25 | PAYMENT RECEIVED | Liabilities:Chase Visa | 1200.00  | none | Cleared   |
| T2  | 2026-04-25 | PAYMENT RECEIVED | Unassigned             | -1200.00 | none | Uncleared |
When the other entry is matched : EntryMatch Vertical
| Ref      | T1 |
| OtherRef | T2 |
Then postings are : PostingRow
| Ref | Date       | Payee     | Account                | Amount   | Memo | Cleared |
| T1  | 2026-04-10 | CHASEBANK | Assets:Checking        | -1200.00 | none | Cleared |
| T1  | 2026-04-10 | CHASEBANK | Liabilities:Chase Visa | 1200.00  | none | Cleared |
Then balances are : AccountBalance CompareOnly
| Account    | RawBalance |
| Unassigned | 0.00       |
# One transaction where there were two. The date is the one the money left, the
# payee is the bank's word for it, and both postings are cleared because both
# banks reported them. The fifteen-day gap was the reason nothing paired it
# automatically and is no reason not to pair it now.

Attributes EntryMatch
Description Matching an Unassigned side against an entry the user picked from
Description another account's register.
| Attribute | Type           | Default |
| Ref       | TransactionRef |         |
| OtherRef  | TransactionRef |         |

Scenario The entries offered to match are the other account's, nearest first
Description Scrolling someone through four hundred rows is not offering a choice.
Description The entries offered are the ones in the account being assigned, with
Description the closest in amount and date at the top.
Given the chart of accounts is : ChartOfAccounts
| Path                   | Type       | Placeholder |
| Assets:Checking        | Bank       | false       |
| Liabilities:Chase Visa | CreditCard | false       |
| Unassigned             | Asset      | false       |
Given postings are : PostingRow
| Ref | Date       | Payee            | Account                | Amount   | Memo | Cleared   |
| T1  | 2026-04-10 | CHASEBANK        | Assets:Checking        | -1200.00 | none | Cleared   |
| T1  | 2026-04-10 | CHASEBANK        | Unassigned             | 1200.00  | none | Uncleared |
| T2  | 2026-04-25 | PAYMENT RECEIVED | Liabilities:Chase Visa | 1200.00  | none | Cleared   |
| T2  | 2026-04-25 | PAYMENT RECEIVED | Unassigned             | -1200.00 | none | Uncleared |
| T3  | 2026-04-26 | SOME SHOP        | Liabilities:Chase Visa | -45.00   | none | Cleared   |
| T3  | 2026-04-26 | SOME SHOP        | Expenses:Uncategorized | 45.00    | none | Uncleared |
When entries offered for : AccountAssignment Vertical
| Ref     | T1                     |
| Account | Liabilities:Chase Visa |
Then entries offered are : OfferedEntry
| Ref | Date       | Payee            | Amount  |
| T2  | 2026-04-25 | PAYMENT RECEIVED | 1200.00 |
| T3  | 2026-04-26 | SOME SHOP        | -45.00  |
# Both are in the card, so both can be chosen; the one whose amount is equal and
# opposite to the unassigned side is first, because it is the one meant nine
# times in ten.

Attributes OfferedEntry
| Attribute | Type           | Default    |
| Ref       | TransactionRef |            |
| Date      | Date           | 2024-01-01 |
| Payee     | PayeeName      | none       |
| Amount    | Money          | 0.00       |

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Naming a transaction
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

BusinessRule A transaction is named when it is created and keeps that name
Description The name is the moment it was created: the date and the time to the
Description millisecond. The generator waits if the clock has not moved since the
Description last name it gave out, so no two can be the same, and a name sorts
Description into the order things were entered.
Details The name is given once, by whatever creates the transaction, and never
Details changes. That is the whole of the rule, and the reason for it is that
Details the alternatives cannot promise it. A name taken from where a
Details transaction came from -- a FITID, a CSV row -- is a fact about a file,
Details and a transaction can collect one of those from every kind of file it is
Details ever recognised in. A posting imported from a CSV and later matched in a
Details QFX has a name in each, which is why ImportIds is a collection.
Details Identity cannot be one of several, and it cannot change when a later
Details import learns something, so it is kept apart from all of them.
Details
Details The import identifiers are still kept and still do the work they always
Details did: they are what makes the same file read twice import nothing the
Details second time. They are facts about the transaction rather than its name.
Details
Details The cost is a millisecond for each transaction created in a run. That is
Details nothing while a person types, and about forty-three seconds for the
Details forty-three thousand transactions of the business QIF -- paid once, on
Details the slowest thing the program already does.
Details
Details What it buys is worth more than the second. A transaction can be traced:
Details a name carrying the minute it was entered answers "when did this appear,
Details and what else appeared with it" without anything having had to record it.
Examples: TransactionNaming
| CreatedBy       | TheNameComesFrom              | Notes                              |
| Typing it in    | The clock, to the millisecond | Nothing else is consulted          |
| An OFX import   | The clock, to the millisecond | The FITID is kept, but as a fact   |
| A CSV import    | The clock, to the millisecond | The row id is kept, but as a fact  |
| A QIF migration | The clock, to the millisecond | A millisecond apart, in file order |

Attributes TransactionNaming
| Attribute        | Type   | Default |
| CreatedBy        | String |         |
| TheNameComesFrom | String |         |
| Notes            | String | none    |

Scenario Two transactions created in the same millisecond cannot happen
Description The generator waits for the clock rather than giving out a name it
Description has given before. So transactions created as fast as the program can
Description create them are still a millisecond apart, and in order.
When transactions are created in a row : TransactionsCreated Vertical
| HowMany | 3 |
Then the names are all different
Then the names increase
# Which is why the database can hold the Id unique and never refuse a write
# that a person did nothing wrong to cause.

Attributes TransactionsCreated
| Attribute | Type    | Default |
| HowMany   | Integer | 2       |

DataType TransactionId
Description The name a transaction is stored under: the date and the time it was
Description created, to the millisecond. Sorts into creation order as text.
Uses Text
Examples: ValidValues
| Value              | IsValid | Notes                                     |
| 20261005T194233123 | true    | The fifth of October, to the millisecond  |
| 20240102T000000000 | true    | Midnight is a time like any other         |
|                    | false   | A transaction always has one              |
| T1                 | false   | That is a Ref, which is a different thing |

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The balancing rule
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

BusinessRule Every transaction balances
Description The amounts of the postings of one transaction sum to zero.
Details This is the whole of double entry. It is checked on every commit, on
Details every import, and when a file is opened, because a book that does not
Details balance cannot be trusted to report anything.
Details Amounts below are in cents, and a blank column is a posting that is not
Details there.
Examples: BalanceCheck
| A1    | A2    | A3    | A4   | Total | Balanced | Notes                        |
| 4500  | -4500 |       |      | 0     | true     | An ordinary payment          |
| -4500 | 1000  | 2000  | 1500 | 0     | true     | A split of three categories  |
| 4500  | -4000 |       |      | 500   | false    | Off by five dollars          |
| 4500  |       |       |      | 4500  | false    | One posting is never balanced |
| 0     | 0     |       |      | 0     | true     | A zero transaction balances  |
| -4500 | 4500  | 1000  | -1000 | 0    | true     | Four postings, still zero    |

Attributes BalanceCheck
| Attribute | Type    | Default |
| A1        | Integer | 0       |
| A2        | Integer | 0       |
| A3        | Integer | 0       |
| A4        | Integer | 0       |
| Total     | Integer | 0       |
| Balanced  | Boolean | true    |
| Notes     | String  | none    |

Scenario A transaction with one posting is refused
Given the chart of accounts is : ChartOfAccounts
| Path               | Type    | Placeholder |
| Assets:Checking    | Bank    | false       |
| Expenses:Groceries | Expense | false       |
When postings committed : PostingRow
| Ref | Date       | Account         | Amount |
| T1  | 2024-01-15 | Assets:Checking | -45.00 |
Then rejected because : Rejection
| Reason                                        |
| A transaction needs at least two postings     |

Scenario An unbalanced transaction is refused
Given the chart of accounts is : ChartOfAccounts
| Path               | Type    | Placeholder |
| Assets:Checking    | Bank    | false       |
| Expenses:Groceries | Expense | false       |
When postings committed : PostingRow
| Ref | Date       | Account            | Amount |
| T1  | 2024-01-15 | Assets:Checking    | -45.00 |
| T1  | 2024-01-15 | Expenses:Groceries | 40.00  |
Then rejected because : Rejection
| Reason                                      |
| T1 is out of balance by 5.00                |

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Entering a transaction the way the register does
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

Attributes RegisterEntry
Description What is typed on one line of a register. The register is always
Description opened on one account, which is the Account column here.
Details Payment and Deposit are the two amount columns of the register, and
Details only one of them is filled in. See TransactionRegister.
| Attribute | Type        | Default    |
| Account   | AccountPath |            |
| Date      | Date        | 2024-01-01 |
| Payee     | PayeeName   | none       |
| CheckNo   | CheckNumber | none       |
| Category  | AccountPath | none       |
| Payment   | Money       | 0.00       |
| Deposit   | Money       | 0.00       |
| Memo      | Text        | none       |

BusinessRule A register entry becomes two postings
Description How the Payment and Deposit columns turn into signed amounts.
Details The amount on the register account is the deposit less the payment, and
Details the category takes the opposite amount, so the two sum to zero.
Examples: RegisterEntryToPostings
| Payment | Deposit | AccountAmount | CategoryAmount | Notes            |
| 45.00   | 0.00    | -45.00        | 45.00          | Money out        |
| 0.00    | 1000.00 | 1000.00       | -1000.00       | Money in         |
| 0.00    | 0.00    | 0.00          | 0.00           | An empty line    |
| 45.00   | 10.00   | -35.00        | 35.00          | Both filled: net |

Attributes RegisterEntryToPostings
| Attribute      | Type   | Default |
| Payment        | Money  | 0.00    |
| Deposit        | Money  | 0.00    |
| AccountAmount  | Money  | 0.00    |
| CategoryAmount | Money  | 0.00    |
| Notes          | String | none    |

Scenario A payment entered in a register
Given the chart of accounts is : ChartOfAccounts
| Path               | Type    | Placeholder |
| Assets:Checking    | Bank    | false       |
| Expenses:Groceries | Expense | false       |
When register entry committed : RegisterEntry Vertical
| Account  | Assets:Checking    |
| Date     | 2024-01-15         |
| Payee    | Harris Teeter      |
| Category | Expenses:Groceries |
| Payment  | 45.00              |
Then postings are : PostingRow
| Ref | Date       | Payee         | Account            | Amount | Cleared   |
| T1  | 2024-01-15 | Harris Teeter | Assets:Checking    | -45.00 | Uncleared |
| T1  | 2024-01-15 | Harris Teeter | Expenses:Groceries | 45.00  | Uncleared |

Scenario A deposit entered in a register
Given the chart of accounts is : ChartOfAccounts
| Path            | Type   | Placeholder |
| Assets:Checking | Bank   | false       |
| Income:Salary   | Income | false       |
When register entry committed : RegisterEntry Vertical
| Account  | Assets:Checking |
| Date     | 2024-01-31      |
| Payee    | Acme Payroll    |
| Category | Income:Salary   |
| Deposit  | 3000.00         |
Then postings are : PostingRow
| Ref | Date       | Payee        | Account         | Amount   | Cleared   |
| T1  | 2024-01-31 | Acme Payroll | Assets:Checking | 3000.00  | Uncleared |
| T1  | 2024-01-31 | Acme Payroll | Income:Salary   | -3000.00 | Uncleared |

Scenario A check records its number
Given the chart of accounts is : ChartOfAccounts
| Path                | Type    | Placeholder |
| Assets:Checking     | Bank    | false       |
| Expenses:Home:Rates | Expense | false       |
When register entry committed : RegisterEntry Vertical
| Account  | Assets:Checking     |
| Date     | 2024-02-01          |
| Payee    | County Tax Office   |
| CheckNo  | 2041                |
| Category | Expenses:Home:Rates |
| Payment  | 1250.00             |
Then transaction is : Transaction Vertical
| Ref     | T1                |
| Date    | 2024-02-01        |
| Payee   | County Tax Office |
| CheckNo | 2041              |
| Memo    | none              |

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Transfers
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

DomainTerm Transfer : Transaction
Description A transaction both of whose postings are to real accounts. It is
Description not a separate kind of record: it is recognised by its shape, and
Description that is why it needs no special handling anywhere else.

Scenario A transfer between two accounts
Description The category column of the register holds the other account. One
Description transaction is created, and it appears in both registers.
Given the chart of accounts is : ChartOfAccounts
| Path            | Type | Placeholder |
| Assets:Checking | Bank | false       |
| Assets:Savings  | Bank | false       |
When register entry committed : RegisterEntry Vertical
| Account  | Assets:Checking |
| Date     | 2024-03-01      |
| Category | Assets:Savings  |
| Payment  | 500.00          |
Then postings are : PostingRow
| Ref | Date       | Account         | Amount  | Cleared   |
| T1  | 2024-03-01 | Assets:Checking | -500.00 | Uncleared |
| T1  | 2024-03-01 | Assets:Savings  | 500.00  | Uncleared |
Then transaction is a transfer : TransferCheck Vertical
| IsTransfer | true           |
| FromAccount | Assets:Checking |
| ToAccount  | Assets:Savings |

Attributes TransferCheck
| Attribute   | Type        | Default |
| IsTransfer  | Boolean     | false   |
| FromAccount | AccountPath | none    |
| ToAccount   | AccountPath | none    |

Scenario A payment to a category is not a transfer
Given the chart of accounts is : ChartOfAccounts
| Path               | Type    | Placeholder |
| Assets:Checking    | Bank    | false       |
| Expenses:Groceries | Expense | false       |
When register entry committed : RegisterEntry Vertical
| Account  | Assets:Checking    |
| Date     | 2024-03-01         |
| Category | Expenses:Groceries |
| Payment  | 45.00              |
Then transaction is a transfer : TransferCheck Vertical
| IsTransfer | false |

Scenario A credit card payment is a transfer, not an expense
Description Paying the card moves money between two real accounts. Recording it
Description as an expense would count the spending twice: once when each charge
Description was categorised, and again when the card was paid.
Given the chart of accounts is : ChartOfAccounts
| Path             | Type       | Placeholder |
| Assets:Checking  | Bank       | false       |
| Liabilities:Visa | CreditCard | false       |
When register entry committed : RegisterEntry Vertical
| Account  | Assets:Checking  |
| Date     | 2024-03-05       |
| Payee    | Visa             |
| Category | Liabilities:Visa |
| Payment  | 300.00           |
Then postings are : PostingRow
| Ref | Date       | Payee | Account          | Amount  | Cleared   |
| T1  | 2024-03-05 | Visa  | Assets:Checking  | -300.00 | Uncleared |
| T1  | 2024-03-05 | Visa  | Liabilities:Visa | 300.00  | Uncleared |
Then balances are : AccountBalance
| Account          | RawBalance | DisplayBalance | Notes                |
| Assets:Checking  | -300.00    | -300.00        | Paid out             |
| Liabilities:Visa | 300.00     | -300.00        | Owed by 300.00 less  |

Scenario A transaction may not post to the same account twice
Description Two postings to one account with opposite amounts would change
Description nothing and clear nothing; it is always a mistake.
Given the chart of accounts is : ChartOfAccounts
| Path            | Type | Placeholder |
| Assets:Checking | Bank | false       |
When postings committed : PostingRow
| Ref | Date       | Account         | Amount  |
| T1  | 2024-03-01 | Assets:Checking | -500.00 |
| T1  | 2024-03-01 | Assets:Checking | 500.00  |
Then rejected because : Rejection
| Reason                                                  |
| A transfer must name two different accounts             |

Scenario A posting to a placeholder account is refused
Description A placeholder exists to group its children in a report. Posting to
Description it makes its own balance differ from the total of its children,
Description which is the one thing a tree of accounts must not do.
Given the chart of accounts is : ChartOfAccounts
| Path               | Type    | Placeholder |
| Assets:Checking    | Bank    | false       |
| Expenses:Auto      | Expense | true        |
| Expenses:Auto:Fuel | Expense | false       |
When register entry committed : RegisterEntry Vertical
| Account  | Assets:Checking |
| Date     | 2024-03-01      |
| Category | Expenses:Auto   |
| Payment  | 45.00           |
Then rejected because : Rejection
| Reason                                             |
| Expenses:Auto is a placeholder; choose one below it |

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Splits
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

Attributes SplitLine
Description One category line of a split, as it is typed into the split window.
Details The amount is entered the way the register entry is: positive for money
Details leaving the account being reconciled.
| Attribute | Type        | Default |
| Category  | AccountPath |         |
| Amount    | Money       | 0.00    |
| Memo      | Text        | none    |

Scenario A split across three categories
Description One trip to the hardware store, charged three ways. The register
Description account takes one posting for the total; each category takes its own.
Given the chart of accounts is : ChartOfAccounts
| Path                       | Type    | Placeholder |
| Assets:Checking            | Bank    | false       |
| Expenses:Home:Maintenance  | Expense | false       |
| Expenses:Home:Garden       | Expense | false       |
| Expenses:Auto:Repairs      | Expense | false       |
When split entry committed : RegisterEntry Vertical
| Account | Assets:Checking |
| Date    | 2024-04-02      |
| Payee   | Home Depot      |
| Payment | 184.50          |
When split lines are : SplitLine
| Category                  | Amount | Memo          |
| Expenses:Home:Maintenance | 100.00 | Paint         |
| Expenses:Home:Garden      | 54.50  | Compost       |
| Expenses:Auto:Repairs     | 30.00  | Wiper blades  |
Then postings are : PostingRow
| Ref | Date       | Payee      | Account                   | Amount  | Memo         |
| T1  | 2024-04-02 | Home Depot | Assets:Checking           | -184.50 | none         |
| T1  | 2024-04-02 | Home Depot | Expenses:Home:Maintenance | 100.00  | Paint        |
| T1  | 2024-04-02 | Home Depot | Expenses:Home:Garden      | 54.50   | Compost      |
| T1  | 2024-04-02 | Home Depot | Expenses:Auto:Repairs     | 30.00   | Wiper blades |

Scenario Split lines that do not add up to the total are refused
Description The split window shows the shortfall as it is typed; this is what
Description happens if the entry is committed anyway.
Given the chart of accounts is : ChartOfAccounts
| Path                       | Type    | Placeholder |
| Assets:Checking            | Bank    | false       |
| Expenses:Home:Maintenance  | Expense | false       |
| Expenses:Home:Garden       | Expense | false       |
| Expenses:Auto:Repairs      | Expense | false       |
When split entry committed : RegisterEntry Vertical
| Account | Assets:Checking |
| Date    | 2024-04-02      |
| Payee   | Home Depot      |
| Payment | 184.50          |
When split lines are : SplitLine
| Category                  | Amount |
| Expenses:Home:Maintenance | 100.00 |
| Expenses:Home:Garden      | 54.50  |
Then rejected because : Rejection
| Reason                                     |
| The split is 30.00 short of 184.50         |

Scenario A split with an income line and an expense line
Description A paycheque is the usual case: gross pay in, tax and insurance out,
Description and the net is what reaches the account.
Given the chart of accounts is : ChartOfAccounts
| Path                   | Type    | Placeholder |
| Assets:Checking        | Bank    | false       |
| Income:Salary          | Income  | false       |
| Expenses:Taxes:Federal | Expense | false       |
| Expenses:Insurance     | Expense | false       |
When split entry committed : RegisterEntry Vertical
| Account | Assets:Checking |
| Date    | 2024-04-15      |
| Payee   | Acme Payroll    |
| Deposit | 3550.00         |
When split lines are : SplitLine
| Category               | Amount   | Memo       |
| Income:Salary          | -5000.00 | Gross pay  |
| Expenses:Taxes:Federal | 1200.00  | Withheld   |
| Expenses:Insurance     | 250.00   | Premium    |
Then postings are : PostingRow
| Ref | Date       | Payee        | Account                | Amount   | Memo      |
| T1  | 2024-04-15 | Acme Payroll | Assets:Checking        | 3550.00  | none      |
| T1  | 2024-04-15 | Acme Payroll | Income:Salary          | -5000.00 | Gross pay |
| T1  | 2024-04-15 | Acme Payroll | Expenses:Taxes:Federal | 1200.00  | Withheld  |
| T1  | 2024-04-15 | Acme Payroll | Expenses:Insurance     | 250.00   | Premium   |
Then balances are : AccountBalance
| Account                | RawBalance | DisplayBalance | Notes            |
| Assets:Checking        | 3550.00    | 3550.00        | Net pay          |
| Income:Salary          | -5000.00   | 5000.00        | Gross, as earned |
| Expenses:Taxes:Federal | 1200.00    | 1200.00        |                  |
| Expenses:Insurance     | 250.00     | 250.00         |                  |

Scenario A split line may transfer to a real account
Description Part of the paycheque going straight to savings is a split line
Description naming an account rather than a category. Nothing special is needed:
Description a posting does not care which kind of account it names.
Given the chart of accounts is : ChartOfAccounts
| Path            | Type   | Placeholder |
| Assets:Checking | Bank   | false       |
| Assets:Savings  | Bank   | false       |
| Income:Salary   | Income | false       |
When split entry committed : RegisterEntry Vertical
| Account | Assets:Checking |
| Date    | 2024-04-15      |
| Payee   | Acme Payroll    |
| Deposit | 2800.00         |
When split lines are : SplitLine
| Category       | Amount   | Memo            |
| Income:Salary  | -3000.00 | Gross pay       |
| Assets:Savings | 200.00   | Direct to saver |
Then postings are : PostingRow
| Ref | Date       | Payee        | Account         | Amount   | Memo            |
| T1  | 2024-04-15 | Acme Payroll | Assets:Checking | 2800.00  | none            |
| T1  | 2024-04-15 | Acme Payroll | Income:Salary   | -3000.00 | Gross pay       |
| T1  | 2024-04-15 | Acme Payroll | Assets:Savings  | 200.00   | Direct to saver |

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# When the other side is not known
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

Scenario A transaction with no category posts to Uncategorized
Description An import knows one side of a transaction and often not the other.
Description Rather than refuse it, or leave the book out of balance, the other
Description side goes to an Uncategorized account chosen by the sign, so that
Description reports stay readable and the work left to do is visible in one
Description place. See ImportOfx.
Given the chart of accounts is : ChartOfAccounts
| Path            | Type | Placeholder |
| Assets:Checking | Bank | false       |
When register entry committed : RegisterEntry Vertical
| Account | Assets:Checking |
| Date    | 2024-05-01      |
| Payee   | Unknown Shop    |
| Payment | 23.75           |
Then postings are : PostingRow
| Ref | Date       | Payee        | Account                  | Amount |
| T1  | 2024-05-01 | Unknown Shop | Assets:Checking          | -23.75 |
| T1  | 2024-05-01 | Unknown Shop | Expenses:Uncategorized   | 23.75  |

Scenario An uncategorised deposit posts to income
Given the chart of accounts is : ChartOfAccounts
| Path            | Type | Placeholder |
| Assets:Checking | Bank | false       |
When register entry committed : RegisterEntry Vertical
| Account | Assets:Checking |
| Date    | 2024-05-02      |
| Payee   | Unknown Source  |
| Deposit | 500.00          |
Then postings are : PostingRow
| Ref | Date       | Payee          | Account              | Amount  |
| T1  | 2024-05-02 | Unknown Source | Assets:Checking      | 500.00  |
| T1  | 2024-05-02 | Unknown Source | Income:Uncategorized | -500.00 |

BusinessRule Which Uncategorized account the other side takes
Description Chosen by the sign of the known posting, so an expense never shows
Description as negative income.
Examples: UncategorizedChoice
| KnownAmount | OtherAccount           | Notes                    |
| -23.75      | Expenses:Uncategorized | Money left the account   |
| 500.00      | Income:Uncategorized   | Money arrived            |
| 0.00        | Expenses:Uncategorized | A zero amount; by convention |

Attributes UncategorizedChoice
| Attribute    | Type        | Default |
| KnownAmount  | Money       | 0.00    |
| OtherAccount | AccountPath |         |
| Notes        | String      | none    |

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Changing and removing
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

Scenario Changing the amount of a two-posting transaction moves both sides
Description With exactly two postings the other side is unambiguous, so editing
Description the amount in the register keeps the transaction balanced without
Description asking anything.
Given the chart of accounts is : ChartOfAccounts
| Path               | Type    | Placeholder |
| Assets:Checking    | Bank    | false       |
| Expenses:Groceries | Expense | false       |
Given postings are : PostingRow
| Ref | Date       | Payee         | Account            | Amount |
| T1  | 2024-01-15 | Harris Teeter | Assets:Checking    | -45.00 |
| T1  | 2024-01-15 | Harris Teeter | Expenses:Groceries | 45.00  |
When amount changed : AmountChange Vertical
| Ref     | T1    |
| Account | Assets:Checking |
| Payment | 52.10 |
Then postings are : PostingRow
| Ref | Date       | Payee         | Account            | Amount |
| T1  | 2024-01-15 | Harris Teeter | Assets:Checking    | -52.10 |
| T1  | 2024-01-15 | Harris Teeter | Expenses:Groceries | 52.10  |

Attributes AmountChange
| Attribute | Type           | Default |
| Ref       | TransactionRef |         |
| Account   | AccountPath    |         |
| Payment   | Money          | 0.00    |
| Deposit   | Money          | 0.00    |

Scenario Changing the amount of a split does not guess which line to adjust
Description With three or more postings there is no one other side, so the
Description shortfall is reported and the split window is opened instead.
Given the chart of accounts is : ChartOfAccounts
| Path                      | Type    | Placeholder |
| Assets:Checking           | Bank    | false       |
| Expenses:Home:Maintenance | Expense | false       |
| Expenses:Home:Garden      | Expense | false       |
Given postings are : PostingRow
| Ref | Date       | Account                   | Amount  |
| T1  | 2024-04-02 | Assets:Checking           | -154.50 |
| T1  | 2024-04-02 | Expenses:Home:Maintenance | 100.00  |
| T1  | 2024-04-02 | Expenses:Home:Garden      | 54.50   |
When amount changed : AmountChange Vertical
| Ref     | T1              |
| Account | Assets:Checking |
| Payment | 200.00          |
Then rejected because : Rejection
| Reason                                                  |
| T1 is a split; the split is 45.50 short of 200.00       |

Scenario Changing the category of one side
Given the chart of accounts is : ChartOfAccounts
| Path               | Type    | Placeholder |
| Assets:Checking    | Bank    | false       |
| Expenses:Groceries | Expense | false       |
| Expenses:Dining    | Expense | false       |
Given postings are : PostingRow
| Ref | Date       | Account            | Amount |
| T1  | 2024-01-15 | Assets:Checking    | -45.00 |
| T1  | 2024-01-15 | Expenses:Groceries | 45.00  |
When category changed : CategoryChange Vertical
| Ref         | T1                 |
| FromAccount | Expenses:Groceries |
| ToAccount   | Expenses:Dining    |
Then postings are : PostingRow
| Ref | Date       | Account         | Amount |
| T1  | 2024-01-15 | Assets:Checking | -45.00 |
| T1  | 2024-01-15 | Expenses:Dining | 45.00  |

Attributes CategoryChange
| Attribute   | Type           | Default |
| Ref         | TransactionRef |         |
| FromAccount | AccountPath    |         |
| ToAccount   | AccountPath    |         |

Scenario Deleting a transaction removes every posting of it
Description Removing one side would leave the book out of balance, so a delete
Description is always of the whole transaction, from whichever register it is
Description asked for.
Given the chart of accounts is : ChartOfAccounts
| Path               | Type    | Placeholder |
| Assets:Checking    | Bank    | false       |
| Expenses:Groceries | Expense | false       |
Given postings are : PostingRow
| Ref | Date       | Account            | Amount |
| T1  | 2024-01-15 | Assets:Checking    | -45.00 |
| T1  | 2024-01-15 | Expenses:Groceries | 45.00  |
| T2  | 2024-01-16 | Assets:Checking    | -12.00 |
| T2  | 2024-01-16 | Expenses:Groceries | 12.00  |
When transaction deleted : TransactionSelect
| Ref |
| T1  |
Then postings are : PostingRow
| Ref | Date       | Account            | Amount |
| T2  | 2024-01-16 | Assets:Checking    | -12.00 |
| T2  | 2024-01-16 | Expenses:Groceries | 12.00  |

Scenario A reconciled posting warns before it is changed
Description A finished reconciliation agreed with a statement. Changing a posting
Description inside it breaks that agreement, so it is allowed but not quietly.
Given the chart of accounts is : ChartOfAccounts
| Path               | Type    | Placeholder |
| Assets:Checking    | Bank    | false       |
| Expenses:Groceries | Expense | false       |
Given postings are : PostingRow
| Ref | Date       | Account            | Amount | Cleared    |
| T1  | 2024-01-15 | Assets:Checking    | -45.00 | Reconciled |
| T1  | 2024-01-15 | Expenses:Groceries | 45.00  | Uncleared  |
When amount changed : AmountChange Vertical
| Ref     | T1              |
| Account | Assets:Checking |
| Payment | 52.10           |
Then warned that : Warning
| Message                                                             |
| This posting is reconciled; changing it will alter a reconciled balance |

Attributes Warning
| Attribute | Type | Default |
| Message   | Text |         |

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Clearing
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

Scenario The two sides of a transfer clear separately
Description Money leaves checking on one statement and arrives in savings on
Description another, often days apart. One posting may be cleared while the
Description other is not, which is why the status is on the posting.
Given the chart of accounts is : ChartOfAccounts
| Path            | Type | Placeholder |
| Assets:Checking | Bank | false       |
| Assets:Savings  | Bank | false       |
Given postings are : PostingRow
| Ref | Date       | Account         | Amount  | Cleared   |
| T1  | 2024-03-01 | Assets:Checking | -500.00 | Uncleared |
| T1  | 2024-03-01 | Assets:Savings  | 500.00  | Uncleared |
When posting cleared : PostingSelect Vertical
| Ref     | T1              |
| Account | Assets:Checking |
Then postings are : PostingRow
| Ref | Date       | Account         | Amount  | Cleared   |
| T1  | 2024-03-01 | Assets:Checking | -500.00 | Cleared   |
| T1  | 2024-03-01 | Assets:Savings  | 500.00  | Uncleared |

Attributes PostingSelect
| Attribute | Type           | Default |
| Ref       | TransactionRef |         |
| Account   | AccountPath    |         |
